The {{nihongo|'''Okami Metro'''|岡見メトロ|Okami Metoro}} is a major [[rapid transit system]] in the [[Okami|Okami Metropolitan Area]] of [[Okaiken]], operated by [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]], and originally opened in 1935 as the Okami Municipal Subway. It is the largest underground transit network in Okaiken and one of the most extensively used daily transit systems in the nation.

Okami Metro forms an integral part of the extensive mass transit system of Greater Okami, operating 12 subway lines and one [[automated guideway transit]] (AGT) line known as the "New Tram." As of 2026, the network comprises {{cvt|512.0|km|mi|abbr=on}} of track and serves 413 stations across the metropolitan area. The system transports approximately 20.3 million passengers daily, functioning as the primary urban transport backbone for [[Okami]] and connecting it to surrounding municipalities through its connections with many private rail operators in the area. The network's daily ridership places it among the busiest metro systems in the world.

Okami Metro is legally classified partially under the Tram Act and partially under the Railway Business Act, though it operates entirely as a heavy rail metro system. Major routes include the [[Miyoshi Line]], which serves as the primary north-south artery beneath Miyoshi Boulevard, and the [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]], which bisects the city east-to-west. The system is closely integrated with regional rail networks, including [[Okaiken Railways]] (OR) and private railways such as [[Okaden|Okami Electric Railway (Okaden)]], allowing for seamless through-service on several lines. The [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]], the longest line in the system at {{cvt|64.2|km|mi|abbr=on}}, connects the western suburbs to the eastern industrial zones through direct through-running with Okaiken Railways commuter lines.

Following the privatization of the [[Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau]] on April 1, 2018, the system has undergone significant modernization, including the introduction of the Satori-AI integrated passenger management system, the rollout of new rolling stock, and the refurbishment of major stations. The flagship [[Okami Metro 4000 series|4000 series]] trains, introduced in 2023, feature a distinctive futuristic design with hexagonal windows and onboard Wi-Fi integrated with the city's Satori Network, and were specifically commissioned to serve as the face of the transport network for the upcoming [[Expo]].

==History==

===Early transit and tram era (1905–1930)===

In 1905, the Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau began operating its municipal tram system. As the Imperial capital's industry expanded rapidly following the [[Sanese-Relastovan War]], the streetcar network grew to cover most of the city, providing essential connectivity between the growing industrial districts, commercial centers, and residential neighborhoods. The tram system was initially hailed as a symbol of Okami's modernization, and by the 1910s, it carried hundreds of thousands of passengers daily across dozens of routes.

However, by the 1920s, surface congestion had become a serious problem. The rapid growth of the city's population, combined with the increasing prevalence of automobile traffic and horse-drawn wagons, led to chronic delays on the street-level tram network. Average tram speeds dropped to below {{cvt|10|km/h|mph|abbr=on}} in the city center, and the Municipal Transportation Bureau began studying grade-separated solutions as early as 1927. Several proposals were considered, including elevated railways and underground subways, before the decision was made to construct a subway line beneath Miyoshi Boulevard, one of the city's busiest thoroughfares.

===First subway and prewar expansion (1930–1940)===

Construction of the first subway line began in 1930. The first section of what is now the [[Miyoshi Line]] opened on June 15, 1935, operating between [[Okami Chūō Station|Okami Chūō]] and [[Hozumi-Oiwake Station|Hozumi Oiwake]]. It was the first publicly operated subway in Okaiken and only the second underground railway in the nation. The opening was celebrated with large public ceremonies, and the line proved immediately popular, carrying over 100,000 passengers on its first day of operation.

The initial rolling stock, designated the [[Okami Metro 0 series|0 series]], was built by local manufacturers and featured wooden interiors with steel exteriors. The success of the first segment prompted plans for further expansion, including an east-west line and extensions to the Miyoshi Line toward the densely inhabited island of [[Tamashima]]. However, these plans were disrupted by the outbreak of the [[Great Adonian War]].

===Wartime and postwar recovery (1940–1960)===

Construction to connect the Miyoshi Line to [[Tamashima]] was halted during the [[Great Adonian War]], as resources and labor were diverted to the war effort. The existing subway infrastructure suffered damage from aerial bombing, particularly in the central city stations, but the tunnels themselves largely survived. Following the war, the Municipal Transportation Bureau focused on repairing existing infrastructure and restoring tram services, which remained the primary mode of public transit in the immediate postwar years.

The postwar economic recovery brought renewed urgency to subway expansion. By the mid-1950s, Okami's population had swelled dramatically, and the tram network was once again overwhelmed. In 1958, the city approved a comprehensive subway expansion plan that would add several new lines over the following two decades. The [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]] was the first to be constructed under this plan, opening in 1961 as the system's second line. The second-generation [[Okami Metro 1000 series|1000 series]] rolling stock was introduced on this line, featuring all-steel construction and significantly higher capacity than the original 0 series.

===Rapid expansion era (1960–1990)===

The 1960s and 1970s saw the most rapid period of expansion in the system's history. The [[Asahi Line]] opened in 1965, followed by the [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]] in 1967, the [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]] in 1969, and the [[Tokuyama Line]] in 1969. By 1970, a grid of six lines had been established, forming the basic skeleton of the modern network. The [[Shitayanagi Line]] opened in 1974, and the [[Tedorigawa Line]] in 1981, bringing the total to eight lines.

The third-generation [[Okami Metro 2000 series|2000 series]] rolling stock was introduced in 1981, featuring air conditioning and improved passenger information systems. The same year, the [[Nankō Port Town Line]] opened as the system's first automated guideway transit line, using the [[Okami Metro 100 series|100 series]] AGT rolling stock to serve the reclaimed port area in Okami Bay. The [[Hitotsubashi Line]] opened in 1988, providing a crucial diagonal connection across the network.

In 1990, the [[Nagata Line]] was opened as the system's first line to utilize [[linear motor]] technology, allowing for smaller tunnel diameters and sharper curves in the dense older districts of the city. The [[Okami Metro 3000 series|3000 series]] linear motor trains were specifically developed for this line. The [[Tokushige Line]] followed in 1995, extending the network further into the southern suburbs.

===Modern era and privatization (2000–present)===

The [[Imaichi Line]], opened in 2006, was the last new subway line to be added to the network, also utilizing linear motor technology. By this time, the system had grown to 12 subway lines and one AGT line, serving over 300 stations. However, the Municipal Transportation Bureau faced mounting financial pressures, with operating costs rising and fare revenues insufficient to cover maintenance and expansion.

On April 1, 2018, the system was privatized as [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]], ending over 80 years of direct municipal management. The privatization was accompanied by a comprehensive modernization program, including the introduction of the Satori-AI integrated passenger management system, the rollout of the new [[Okami Metro 5000 series|5000 series]] rolling stock, and the refurbishment of the 2000 series and 100 series fleets. The [[Okami Metro 6000 series|6000 series]] was introduced in 2020, followed by the flagship [[Okami Metro 4000 series|4000 series]] in 2023 and the [[Okami Metro 7000 series|7000 series]] in 2025. The 3000 series linear motor trains are currently being retired and replaced with 5000 series stock.

