==Stations and accessibility==

Okami Metro stations are renowned for their integration with the city's environment. Following the 2018 privatization, major hubs like [[Okami Chuo Station|Okami Chūō]], [[Otemachi Station|Otemachi]], and [[Hozumi-Oiwake Station|Hozumi-Oiwake]] were renovated to include massive LED displays, biometric ticketing gates, and improved wayfinding systems. The Satori-AI passenger management system, deployed across the network, provides real-time service information, crowd density monitoring, and personalized route guidance through the Okami Metro mobile application.

[[Universal accessibility|Universal accessibility]] is a standard across all 413 stations. The "One Route" initiative, launched in 2019, ensures that there is at least one barrier-free path from street level to the platform at every station in the network. This includes [[elevator]]s, [[wheelchair ramp]]s, [[tactile paving]], and [[platform screen door]]s. All stations are equipped with [[accessible toilet]]s and [[multipurpose rooms]] for passengers with special needs. As of 2026, 100% of stations have platform screen doors installed, making Okami Metro one of the few systems in the world to achieve full platform edge door coverage.

Station numbering follows a systematic scheme using the {{tl|OMSN}} (Okami Metro Station Number) format, with each station assigned a line code letter and a two-digit sequential number. For example, {{OMSN|M|01}} denotes the first station on the Miyoshi Line, and {{OMSN|P|09}} denotes the ninth station on the Nankō Port Town Line. This system was introduced in 2018 and is displayed on all station signage, train destination indicators, and passenger information materials.

